findings.body:
`CleanupHandles` runs inside the finally block, before `ExitReadLock`/`ExitWriteLock`, and throws a `Win32Exception` when `SetHandleInformation` fails. It also calls `DangerousRelease` first, so a handle disposed concurrently can make the marshaller throw `ObjectDisposedException`.

Any such throw has four effects:
- the read lock is never exited, so every later `Process.Start` deadlocks;
- the remaining handles are never released;
- `commandLine` is never disposed;
- a successfully created child process is leaked while its exception is masked.

Fix: never throw from cleanup (ignore or log failures), clear the flag before releasing the ref, and release the lock in an outer finally.
